# Gibson fleet profile — format version 1
# Copy this file to a *local* path (outside the repo, or under local/ which is
# gitignored) and fill in absolute paths for your machine. Do NOT commit
# private home paths, credentials, or a live product issue queue.
#
# Load with:
#   FLEET_PROFILE=/absolute/path/to/this-file /absolute/path/to/the-gibson/scripts/loop-fleet.sh --start
#   # or:
#   /absolute/path/to/the-gibson/scripts/loop-fleet.sh --profile /absolute/path/to/this-file --status
#
# Spec: templates/fleet/README.md  ·  driver: scripts/loop-fleet.sh  ·  issues #139 / #141

version=1
name=example-target

# Absolute path to the target repository checkout (canonical, usually clean main).
repo=/absolute/path/to/your-target-repo

# Expected GitHub owner/repo — must match `git remote get-url origin`.
slug=owner/your-target-repo

# Optional: absolute path to the Gibson clone that provides scripts/loop.sh.
# Default: parent of the loop-fleet.sh you invoked.
# gibson=/absolute/path/to/the-gibson

# Optional: where long-lived lane-* worktrees and logs live (absolute).
# fleet_dir=/absolute/path/to/fleet-worktrees
# log_dir=/absolute/path/to/fleet-logs

# Optional global default builder when a lane omits its route field.
# runner=grok
# error_budget=4
# deadline_seconds=79200

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lanes — one `lane=` line each. Pipe-separated fields:
#   id|ordered-issue-queue|exclusive-scope|intent[|ordered-runner-route]
#
# - id: unique, [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9_-]*  (never wt-* — those names are disposable)
# - queue: comma-separated positive issue numbers, work order left → right
# - scope: space-separated path/glob tokens; must be disjoint across lanes
# - intent: free text the builder re-reads every hat
# - ordered-runner-route (#141): optional 5th field — comma-separated runner
#   CLIs, primary first, explicit fallbacks after. Empty → global runner=.
#   Only declared runners may be selected. Fail over only on readiness failure.
#
# Flat-rate-first (docs/15): grind lanes should list flat-rate pools first
# (e.g. grok) and metered/frontier providers only at escalation positions.
# The driver preserves operator order; it does not reorder by price or inspect
# billing/plans/keys.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Example: Gibson self-dogfood / Autonomy Readiness (issue #96 and friends).
# Replace issue numbers with open, non-gated items from the live backlog.
# Grind lane: flat-rate primary, subscription fallback only if primary is not ready.
lane=docs|96|docs/** playbooks/** memory/dogfood/**|Overnight dogfood + operator playbooks (Autonomy Readiness / #96). Stay out of scripts/**.|grok,codex
lane=harness|89|scripts/**|Self-gate and harness sensors only. Do not edit docs/** or playbooks/**.|grok

# Chatterbuilt-shaped illustration (synthetic issue ids — NOT a live queue).
# Migrate your real Chatterbuilt LANES into a local profile that is never
# committed to the generic Gibson tree. Scopes show the disjoint-lane idea:
#   lane=mcpcore|1001,1002|apps/mcp/lib/** apps/mcp/app/**|MCP server work|grok,codex
#   lane=growth|1003|marketing/app/** marketing/components/**|Marketing surface|grok
#   lane=docs|1004|docs/**|Docs only|grok
